  <article>
  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="guide-heading">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:800px">
      <span class="badge">The Complete Guide</span>
      <h1 id="guide-heading" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,3.6rem);margin-bottom:1rem">Paper Honeycomb Board: The Complete Guide</h1>
      <p style="color:#666;line-height:1.7;font-size:1.05rem">
        Everything buyers, engineers, and packaging managers need to know about paper honeycomb
        board — what it is, how it's made, how strong it really is, and when it beats plywood,
        plastic, and corrugated. Written by the manufacturing team at phb, Malaysia.
      </p>
      <nav aria-label="Table of contents" class="toc">
        <strong>In this guide:</strong>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-is">What is paper honeycomb board?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-made">How is it made?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#strength">How strong is it?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#ispm15">Why is it ISPM-15 exempt?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#applications">What is it used for?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#comparison">Honeycomb vs plywood vs plastic vs corrugated</a></li>
          <li><a href="#choosing">How to choose the right board</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section guide-body">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:800px">

      <h2 id="what-is">What Is Paper Honeycomb Board?</h2>
      <p>
        Paper honeycomb board is a <strong>sandwich material</strong>: a core of kraft paper formed
        into hexagonal cells, glued between two flat paper liners. The hexagon — the same geometry
        bees evolved for maximum storage with minimum wax — distributes load across cell walls so
        efficiently that a board that is mostly <em>air</em> behaves structurally like solid timber.
      </p>
      <p>
        The result is one of the best strength-to-weight ratios of any packaging material:
        a standard honeycomb pallet weighs <strong>around 5kg</strong> where its plywood equivalent
        weighs <strong>around 25kg</strong> — up to 80% lighter for comparable static duty.
        The same principle is used in aircraft floors and race car panels (with aluminium or aramid
        cores); paper honeycomb brings that engineering to packaging economics.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how-made">How Is Honeycomb Board Made?</h2>
      <p>The manufacturing process has five steps:</p>
      <ol class="guide-list">
        <li><strong>Gluing:</strong> kraft paper sheets receive parallel glue lines, offset between layers.</li>
        <li><strong>Stacking &amp; pressing:</strong> hundreds of sheets are stacked and bonded into a block.</li>
        <li><strong>Slicing:</strong> the block is cut into slices at the target core thickness.</li>
        <li><strong>Expanding:</strong> each slice is stretched like an accordion — the offset glue lines open into hexagonal cells — then dried to hold the shape.</li>
        <li><strong>Laminating:</strong> the expanded core is glued between top and bottom liners, creating the rigid sandwich board.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Board properties are tuned through <strong>cell size</strong> (smaller cells = more walls =
        higher strength), <strong>core thickness</strong> (10mm to 100mm+), <strong>paper grammage</strong>,
        and <strong>liner grade</strong>. That's why honeycomb spans everything from lightweight void
        fill to pallet decks carrying industrial machinery.
      </p>

      <h2 id="strength">How Strong Is Paper Honeycomb Board?</h2>
      <p>
        Stronger than most first-time buyers expect. Compression strength comes from the cell walls
        loaded on-edge — the same reason a sheet of paper that flops flat can support a book when
        rolled into a tube. Engineered honeycomb panels handle compression forces around
        <strong>12kN</strong>, and pallet-grade builds carry the same static loads as plywood decks
        for typical export cargo.
      </p>
      <p>Three variables drive load capacity:</p>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li><strong>Cell size:</strong> 8–25mm typical; smaller cells for higher point loads.</li>
        <li><strong>Core thickness:</strong> thicker cores resist bending over longer spans.</li>
        <li><strong>Paper grade:</strong> higher-grammage kraft and multi-ply liners for heavy duty.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        One honest caveat: standard board is <strong>not waterproof</strong>. Saturated paper loses
        strength, so wet-transit applications use moisture-resistant coatings or PE lamination, and
        cargo is film-wrapped as with any packaging. For continuously wet environments, honeycomb
        is the wrong material — that's plywood's remaining stronghold.
      </p>

      <h2 id="ispm15">Why Is Honeycomb Board ISPM-15 Exempt?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>ISPM-15</strong> is the international phytosanitary standard requiring solid wood
        packaging (pallets, crates, dunnage) to be heat-treated or fumigated and stamped, because
        raw wood can carry invasive pests. <strong>Processed paper is biologically inert</strong> —
        the pulping and pressing process eliminates any pest pathway — so paper honeycomb packaging
        is fully exempt worldwide.
      </p>
      <p>In practice, switching from wood to honeycomb means:</p>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>No heat treatment or methyl bromide fumigation fees</li>
        <li>No IPPC stamps or phytosanitary certificates</li>
        <li>No consignments rejected or quarantined at the border for non-compliant wood</li>
        <li>Same-day shipping readiness — no treatment scheduling</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="applications">What Is Honeycomb Board Used For?</h2>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li><strong><a href="/honeycomb-pallet/">Paper pallets</a></strong> — export shipping's biggest tare-weight win: ~5kg per pallet, ISPM-15 exempt.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="/honeycomb-box/">Heavy-duty boxes &amp; crates</a></strong> — wooden crate replacement for machinery, electronics, and furniture.</li>
        <li><strong>Protective packaging</strong> — edge protectors, corner blocks, void fillers, and cushioning inserts.</li>
        <li><strong>Furniture cores</strong> — most flat-pack furniture uses honeycomb cores inside doors, desktops, and shelving.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="/honeycomb-panels/">Display &amp; architectural panels</a></strong> — exhibition stands, retail displays, stage sets, and 1:1 prototypes.</li>
        <li><strong>Automotive &amp; white goods dunnage</strong> — part separators and transport frames on production lines.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="comparison">Honeycomb vs Plywood vs Plastic vs Corrugated</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Material comparison" tabindex="0">
        <table class="compare-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Property</th>
              <th scope="col" class="col-phb">Honeycomb</th>
              <th scope="col">Plywood</th>
              <th scope="col">Plastic</th>
              <th scope="col">Corrugated</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Pallet weight</th>
              <td class="col-phb"><strong>~5KG</strong></td>
              <td>~25KG</td>
              <td>~18KG</td>
              <td>~4KG (light duty)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">ISPM-15</th>
              <td class="col-phb"><strong>Exempt</strong></td>
              <td>Required</td>
              <td>Exempt</td>
              <td>Exempt</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Heavy loads</th>
              <td class="col-phb"><strong>Yes (engineered)</strong></td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>Limited</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Recyclability</th>
              <td class="col-phb"><strong>100% curbside</strong></td>
              <td>Difficult / landfill</td>
              <td>Specialised only</td>
              <td>100% curbside</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Wet environments</th>
              <td class="col-phb">Coated builds only</td>
              <td><strong>Good</strong></td>
              <td><strong>Best</strong></td>
              <td>Poor</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Unit cost</th>
              <td class="col-phb"><strong>Low–medium</strong></td>
              <td>Medium</td>
              <td>High</td>
              <td>Lowest</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Rule of thumb: <strong>corrugated</strong> for light cartons, <strong>plastic</strong> for
        closed-loop reuse in wet plants, <strong>plywood</strong> for permanently wet or
        decade-reuse duty, and <strong>honeycomb</strong> for everything that ships — especially
        one-way export where tare weight and ISPM-15 paperwork are pure cost.
        See the full <a href="/honeycomb-board-vs-plywood/">honeycomb vs plywood vs plastic comparison</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="choosing">How to Choose the Right Honeycomb Board</h2>
      <ol class="guide-list">
        <li><strong>Start from the cargo:</strong> weight, dimensions, fragility, and stacking plan determine core thickness and cell size.</li>
        <li><strong>Map the journey:</strong> air freight rewards every gram saved; sea freight cares more about compression stacking; humid routes need coated liners.</li>
        <li><strong>Check compliance:</strong> exporting? Honeycomb's ISPM-15 exemption removes the treatment step entirely.</li>
        <li><strong>Prototype at 1:1:</strong> a sample kit with different densities lets your team drop-test before committing a production run.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        That's exactly what our <a href="/contact/">sample kit</a> is for — three densities of
        structural honeycomb plus our finish guide, shipped from our Balakong, Selangor facility
        to anywhere in Malaysia and Singapore.
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is paper honeycomb board?</summary>
          <p>A sandwich material of hexagonal kraft-paper cells laminated between flat liners — plywood-class rigidity at up to 80% less weight.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Is honeycomb board waterproof?</summary>
          <p>Standard board is not. Moisture-resistant coatings or PE lamination are available for humid routes; permanently wet duty suits plywood or plastic better.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Is it really strong enough to replace wood?</summary>
          <p>For most one-way export duty, yes — engineered builds handle ~12kN compression and pallet-grade static loads. Share your cargo specs and we'll engineer the build.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Does it need ISPM-15 treatment?</summary>
          <p>No. Processed paper is biologically inert and exempt worldwide — no fumigation, no stamps, no delays.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Who makes honeycomb board in Malaysia?</summary>
          <p>phb — Quinocycle Sdn Bhd — manufactures in Balakong, Selangor, serving the Klang Valley, KL, Johor Bahru, and Singapore. <a href="/contact/" style="text-decoration:underline">Contact us</a>.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

      <div class="cta-inline">
        <h2>Ready to test it yourself?</h2>
        <p>Get the architect-grade sample kit — three densities, finish guide, delivered fast.</p>
        <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn--green">Get a Paper Honeycomb Board Sample Kit</a>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

